-{"id":"LOAD-BRIDGE-1","title":"[P1] aipp-bridge spawns an unbounded daemon thread per connection \u2014 1018 threads in R state drove host loadavg to 1015.9 and NOTHING alerted for 73 minutes","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":4,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Deep-reflection evidence 2026-09-30 04:00Z (job 6d2a35117904). Per-minute load1_after from ~/.hermes/logs/forkbomb-reaper.jsonl: baseline 5-25 for all of 09-29, then 21:49 -> 36, 22:10 -> 176, 22:24 -> 399, 22:31 -> 514, 22:42 -> 686, 22:49 -> 807, 23:02:13 -> 1015.9 PEAK (linear, ~13 threads/min over 73 min). Culprit was a single PID: aipp-bridge aipp-bridge.service ExecStart script, started 18:41:14, 304% CPU, 1018 threads ALL in R state (ps -eLo stat); /proc/loadavg 1-min equalled the R-thread count exactly (1015.9 vs 1018), so this is a thread SPIN, not a fork storm. Nothing else on the box was running: 2 procs in R outside that PID. No alert fired in any of the 73 minutes: forkbomb-reaper logged killed_total=0 in every sample and only models shell busy-waits/fork storms. The process exited 23:02:57 (no crash line in /tmp/aipp_bridge.log, no OOM in journalctl, 39 G RAM available at the time) and systemd Restart=always respawned it; the new instance held 16 threads and load was 183 and falling by 23:06. The only unbounded thread-spawn site in scripts/game_bridge.py is serve() lines 313-317: `conn, _ = srv.accept()` then `threading.Thread(target=handle, ..., daemon=True).start()` with srv.listen(16), no pool, no cap, never joined. CAVEAT to resolve before fixing: R-state (spinning, 304% CPU) is NOT what a thread blocked in conn.recv would report, so the leaking handler path still has to be identified \u2014 the ramp rate and the single spawn site make the request path the prime suspect. Acceptance criteria: (1) the request path that leaks is named with a reproducible trigger; (2) serve() gains a bounded concurrency model (pool or explicit cap) and threads are joined/closed; (3) a soak that opens N concurrent connections for >=10 min shows thread count bounded and constant; (4) a watchdog asserts no single PID exceeds a thread ceiling and restarts the unit, since the current reaper is blind to this class; (5) the incident is written up as the second load source the reaper cannot see (first: gitleaks guard fanout, 2026-09-29)."}
