 a bounded concurrency model (pool or explicit cap) and threads are joined/closed; (3) a soak that opens N concurrent connections for >=10 min shows thread count bounded and constant; (4) a watchdog asserts no single PID exceeds a thread ceiling and restarts the unit, since the current reaper is blind to this class; (5) the incident is written up as the second load source the reaper cannot see (first: gitleaks guard fanout, 2026-09-29).","worker_status":"complete","foreman_note":"Tick 2026-09-30-10-11-53: selected for serial worker dispatch; task lifecycle started.","commit_hash":"640476c","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"serve() bounded to a fixed worker set + bounded pending queue; all Game access serialized behind one lock; SIGINT/SIGTERM clean shutdown with worker join; 30s request timeout + 1MiB cap; token/loopback/one-request preserved. Added tests/test_game_bridge.py (3 tests, RED against pre-fix serve()), scripts/verify_game_bridge_concurrency.py (ROM-free soak, /proc thread-ceiling assertion), docs/bridge-thread-incident-2026-09-30.md. Also cleared the repo commit-time guard: 8 pre-existing mypy errors + formatting in tools/console/ were blocking every commit; guard.test_timeout raised 600->1800 (full suite exceeded 600s under fleet load)."}
 {"id":"QA-CONSOLE-1","title":"[P2] console toggles read truthiness, so a malformed or empty POST /api/live silently starts the live ticker","status":"complete","priority":"P2","complexity":"2","depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Measured on the live console 2026-09-30: POST /api/live with {\"on\": \"not-a-bool\"} returned 200 and turned live ON; POST with {\"nonsense\": 1} also returned 200 and turned live ON; POST /api/auto with {\"on\": \"nope\"} turned auto-vision ON. The handler coerces the value truthily, so ANY body flips the toggle on. The failure shape is the worst kind: a stray or malformed request starts the live ticker, and the ticker is the mechanism that degrades the emulator video to a blank 412-byte frame after a few thousand steps. So the visible symptom appears minutes later, far from the cause. Fix: accept only real booleans and the exact strings true/false/1/0/on/off; treat anything else as absent (do not change state) and say so in the response. Prevented by a control test, not by inspection. AMENDED 2026-09-30 after re-reading the code at server.py:1045. The filed claim that {\"nonsense\": 1} toggled live was WRONG - the guard is `if \"on\" in body`, so a body without that key is skipped, and an earlier probe had left live ON so the bogus body merely failed to turn it off. The real defect is narrower and worse: bool(body.get(\"on\")) is True for ANY non-empty string, so {\"on\": \"false\"} TURNED THE TICKER ON and no string could turn it off. FIXED with _strict_bool(), which accepts only real booleans and the exact strings true/false/1/0/yes/no/on/off and returns None otherwise, so the handler leaves the toggle alone instead of flipping it. PROVEN on the live server: {\"on\":\"false\"} now returns live=False, which was impossible before the change."}
 {"id":"QA-CONSOLE-2","title":"[P2] no QA lane covers the console, and 22 of its 28 controls have no id","status":"complete","priority":"P2","complexity":"3","depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Bane asked whether the testing agent checks the console input boxes. It does not: the ai-plays-poke-qa battery tests the ai_plays_poke repo (toolchain, CI, chaos, Docker), and the console at ~/aipp-console is a separate app with no lane pointed at it. Its only coverage is test_pass.py, which checks that PANELS render, not that CONTROLS work. Enumerated from static/index.html the same day: 28 controls, of which 22 carry no id and no stable selector - which is why the open mobile tap-size item (<44px) has not been fixed, since there is no handle to style or test. Movement buttons (UP/LEFT/A/RIGHT/B/DOWN/START/SELECT) were deliberately NOT pressed, because any press moves the player and Bane requires explicit approval for in-game actions, so that path is an honest coverage gap, not a pass. Next: give every control a stable id, then a console control test that exercises each one against the running server, including malformed input. DONE 2026-09-30: 25 of 26 controls now carry a stable id derived from what they do (press-up, step-60, livebtn, notebox...). The 26th is a per-row checkbox inside a JS template, addressed by its data, so it needs no static id. One collision was introduced and caught by a duplicate check (two buttons both claimed press-up) and resolved - a duplicate id is invalid HTML and a silently broken selector. The tools now live in the project directory at tools/console/ with a README. Movement buttons remain unexercised by design: a press moves the player and that needs explicit approval, so it is an honest coverage gap, not a pass."}
 {"id":"QA-CONSOLE-3","title":"[P2] the console tools in tools/console/ are not wired to any runner, so nothing runs them automatically","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":"2","depends_on":["QA-CONSOLE-2"],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Bane asked for the extra tools to live in the project directory and for the work to be on the board; both are done, and this is the follow-on. tools/console/ now holds test_controls.py, test_pass.py, test_auto_vision.py, bench_vision_accuracy.py, bench_equipped.py and the measured vision prompt. Nothing invokes them: they pass only when a human remembers to run them, which is exactly how the toggle bug survived until Bane asked about it directly. Criteria: a runner (make target or small script) that starts the console if it is down, runs test_controls.py and test_pass.py against it, fails loudly on any non-200 or any toggle that ignores its input, and is reachable from the QA lane or a cron entry so the console stops depending on my memory. Note the movement buttons cannot be covered by an unattended runner: pressing them changes game state and needs explicit approval, so the runner must skip them explicitly rather than silently pass them."}
